WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:05.130
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:07.430
This is what we have been waiting for.

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:12.610
Earlier, we talked about a neural network
that was able to describe in a full sentence

00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:14.280
what we can see on an image.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.840
And it had done a damn good job at that.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:22.340
Then, we have talked about a technique that
did something really crazy, the exact opposite:

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:26.620
we wrote a sentence, and it created new images
according to that.

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:28.400
This is already incredible.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:33.620
And we can create an algorithm like this by
training not one, but two neural networks:

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:38.780
The first is the generative network, that
creates millions of new images, and the discriminator

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:42.650
network judges whether these are real or fake
images.

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:47.190
The generative network can improve its game
based on the feedback and will create more

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:51.880
and more realistic looking images, while the
discriminator network gets better and better

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:54.610
at telling real images from fake ones.

00:00:54.610 --> 00:01:00.160
Like humans, this rivalry drives both neural
networks towards perfecting their crafts.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:04.180
This architecture is called a generative adversarial
network.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:09.200
It is also like the classical, evergoing arms
race between criminals who create counterfeit

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:15.070
money and the government, which seeks to implement
newer and newer measures to tell a real hundred

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:17.070
dollar bill from a fake one.

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:22.390
The previous generative adversarial networks
were adept at creating new images, but due

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:27.190
to their limitations, their image outputs
were the size of a stamp at best.

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:32.350
And we were wondering, how long until we get
much higher resolution images from such a

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:33.350
system?

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:37.119
Well, I am delighted to say that apparently,
within the same year.

00:01:37.119 --> 00:01:40.940
In this work, a two-stage version of this
architecture is proposed.

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:45.729
The stage 1 network is close to the generative
adversarial network we described.

00:01:45.729 --> 00:01:51.390
And most of the fun happens in the stage 2
network, that takes this rough, low resolution

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:56.700
image and the text description and is told
to correct the defects of the previous output

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.180
and create a higher resolution version of
it.

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:03.860
In the video, the input text description and
the stage-1 results are shown, and building

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:08.229
on that, the higher resolution stage-2 images
are presented.

00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:10.770
And the results are... unreal.

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:15.720
There was a previous article and Two Minute
Papers episode on the unreasonable effectiveness

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:17.620
of recurrent neural networks.

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:20.940
If that is unreasonable effectiveness, then
what is this?

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:26.970
The rate of progress in machine learning research
is unlike any other field I have ever seen.

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:30.240
I honestly can't believe what I am seeing
here.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:35.540
Dear Fellow Scholars, what you see might very
well be history in the making.

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:37.790
Are there still faults in the results?

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:38.970
Of course there are.

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:39.970
Are they perfect?

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:42.349
No, they certainly aren't.

00:02:42.349 --> 00:02:48.270
However, research is all about progress and
it's almost never possible to go from 0 to

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:51.709
a 100% with one new revolutionary idea.

00:02:51.709 --> 00:02:58.819
However, I am sure that in 2017, researchers
will start working on generating full HD animations

00:02:58.819 --> 00:03:01.060
with an improved version of this architecture.

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:05.940
Make sure to have a look at the paper, where
the ideas, challenges, and possible solutions

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:08.050
are very clearly presented.

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:11.220
And for now, I need some time to digest these
results.

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:15.989
Currently I feel like being dropped into the
middle of a science-fiction movie.

00:03:15.989 --> 00:03:19.180
And, this one will be our last video for this
year.

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:24.430
We have had an amazing year with some incredible
growth on the channel, way more of you Fellow

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:28.760
Scholars decided to come with us on our journey
than I would have imagined.

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:33.140
Thank you so much for being a part of Two
Minute Papers, we'll be continuing full steam

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:38.140
ahead next year, and for now, I wish you a
Merry Christmas and happy holidays.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:43.739
2016 was an amazing year for research, and
2017 will be even better.

00:03:43.739 --> 00:03:44.739
Stay tuned!

00:03:44.739 --> 00:03:48.359
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

